    <!-- Permission -->
    <string name="soundmeter_permission_rationale">Measuring needs access to the microphone. Nothing is recorded or stored — each fraction of a second of sound is turned into a number and discarded, and no audio leaves the device.</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_permission_grant">Allow microphone access</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_permission_blocked">Microphone access is turned off for this app. You can switch it back on in Settings.</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_permission_open_settings">Open settings</string>

    <!-- Settings -->
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_title">Calibration and accuracy</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_offset_title">Calibration offset</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_offset_body">Shifts every reading by a fixed amount. To set it honestly, measure a steady sound next to a real sound level meter and move this until the two agree. Guessing at it, or adjusting until the number looks right, does not calibrate anything.</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_offset_value">%1$+.1f dB</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_offset_reset">Reset to zero</string>

    <string name="soundmeter_settings_accuracy_title">How accurate is this?</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_accuracy_body">The filters are built to the IEC 61672-1 weighting curves and are checked against the standard\'s published response at every octave. The absolute level is a different matter: converting the microphone\'s digital signal into decibels needs its sensitivity, Android does not expose that, and it varies by 10–20 dB between phone models. So relative changes are trustworthy — if the reading rises by 10 dB, the room really did get about 10 dB louder — while the absolute figure carries an unknown offset until you set one above. Very loud sounds are also beyond the range of the hardware; the meter says so rather than reporting a number it cannot stand behind.</string>

    <string name="soundmeter_settings_privacy_title">What happens to the audio</string>
    <string name="soundmeter_settings_privacy_body">Nothing is recorded. Sound arrives in fragments of about a fiftieth of a second, each one is reduced to a single level, and the samples are dropped immediately. No audio is written to storage, and none of it leaves the device. The microphone is released the moment you leave this screen — the app does not hold it in the background, and does not ask for the permission that would let it.</string>

